1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,041
Back in Section two, the decision tree gave agent one box among four, and moved on.

2
00:00:05,041 --> 00:00:06,172
That was deliberate.

3
00:00:06,172 --> 00:00:10,524
You needed the other three first, because an agent is what you reach for once

4
00:00:10,524 --> 00:00:14,481
prompting, retrieval, and fine-tuning have all genuinely fallen short.

5
00:00:14,481 --> 00:00:17,420
This section is that fourth box, opened up properly.

6
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:22,055
By the end, you'll know what actually separates an agent from a chatbot with extra

7
00:00:22,055 --> 00:00:26,633
steps, the maths that tells you when a five-step plan is quietly a coin flip, and

8
00:00:26,633 --> 00:00:30,872
how to scope one so a failure costs you an afternoon instead of a headline.

9
00:00:31,422 --> 00:00:36,079
Five ideas, and the same discipline Section three brought to specifying a feature,

10
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:38,238
now aimed at one that acts on its own.

11
00:00:38,238 --> 00:00:43,009
You'll learn what an agent actually is underneath the term, how it reaches your real

12
00:00:43,009 --> 00:00:47,666
tools, and the reliability maths that turns "it worked in the demo" into an actual

13
00:00:47,666 --> 00:00:48,063
number.

14
00:00:48,063 --> 00:00:52,607
Then the design patterns that hold up once real users show up, including when to

15
00:00:52,607 --> 00:00:57,094
reach for several agents instead of one, and the guardrails that keep a mistake

16
00:00:57,094 --> 00:00:58,628
small instead of expensive.

17
00:00:58,628 --> 00:01:03,228
It closes the way Section three did: a scoping worksheet, filled in for real, for

18
00:01:03,228 --> 00:01:05,784
something you'd actually consider automating.
